DELTA=0.2 chi2_95(df=3)=7.8147

analytic affine prediction vs observed pooled margin
  n= 25: predicted=0.768 observed=0.659 ratio=0.859
  n= 50: predicted=1.152 observed=1.056 ratio=0.917
  n=100: predicted=1.919 observed=1.781 ratio=0.928
  n=200: predicted=3.455 observed=3.405 ratio=0.986
  (observed sits below the mean-based prediction because the median of a noncentral chi-square is below its mean; the gap shrinks with n)

least-squares affine fit to observed: margin = 0.258 + 0.01566 * n
  analytic slope 3*DELTA^2/chi2_95 = 0.01536
  residuals: +0.010 +0.016 -0.042 +0.016
  R^2 = 0.9995

power-law fit to observed (rule 2 family): slope = 0.786

local log-log slopes between adjacent n
   25-> 50: observed=0.680 affine=0.585
   50->100: observed=0.754 affine=0.737
  100->200: observed=0.935 affine=0.848
  both climb toward 1: an affine curve has no single exponent, so rule 2's sqrt(n)-vs-n question is undecided by construction.

verdict: margin is affine in n (a + b*n with a>0); the fitted exponent 0.79 is the power-law shadow of that line over 25..200, not evidence of a growth rate between sqrt(n) and n.
